        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>

Rapid fluid shifts are known to occur in the body during transitions between the normal one-g environment of the Earth and the weightless environment of space. These shifts produce changes of unknown magnitude to the body's regulatory mechanisms. With regard to hematological changes, medical studies conducted in support of the Gemini and Apollo programs suggested possible influences of the space-flight environment on red blood cell integrity and the normal regulation of the circulating red blood cell mass. Skylab experiment M115 was specifically designed to investigate, detect, and characterize alterations in red cell shape either during or following extended exposure to the space environment.
<p>

APPROACH:<br>

Blood samples were collected by venipuncture from the Skylab crew and from a ground-based control group of subjects during the pre-, in-, and postflight phase of each mission. All samples were obtained using the In-flight Blood Collection System (IBCS), and processed or stabilized within minutes of collection. Anticoagulants were used to collect samples for some tests.  In-flight samples were collected in Na2EDTA and immediately separated by centrifugation into plasma and cellular phases by the in-flight sample processor, specially designed to operate in a weightless environment. The separated in-flight samples were frozen at -20 degrees Celsius and stored on board until postflight recovery and transfer to the ground laboratory for analysis.
<p>
In addition to measurements of standard hematologic parameters such as total red blood cell population, hemoglobin, and hematocrit, the experiment included morphological studies of red blood cells. Hemoglobin concentrations were measured during flight using a hemoglobin meter in conjunction with each in-flight blood draw. Postflight, duplicate measurements of the hemoglobin concentration measured in-flight were made using the cyanmethemoglobin method and compared to hemoglobin meter measurements. The active and passive components of potassium transport into red blood cells were  determined using a ouabain inhibition, 86Rb substitution technique.
<p>
For morphological studies, the red blood cells from blood samples collected during the pre-, in-, and postflight phases of each Skylab mission were fixed in a fixative solution containing 1.0 milliliters of 0.5 percent glutaraldehyde (pH 7.4). Time in the fixative varied from 1 hour for pre- and postflight samples to 1, 2, 24, 57, and 81 days for in-flight samples (this variation had no effect on cell morphology). Following fixation, the cells were dehydrated with ethanol and then 50 percent amyl acetate/50 percent ethanol. The samples were critical point dried in a Denton critical point drying apparatus. The samples were then coated with a gold/palladium mixture and examined under a scanning electron microscope. The cells were categorized according to the cell shapes listed below:<br>
Discocyte (disc-shaped, normal)<br>
Leptocyte (thin, flat)<br>
Codocyte (bell-shaped)<br>
Stomatocyte (single concavity)<br>
Knizocyte (pinched, triconcave)<br>
Echinocyte (spiny)<br>

<p>

RESULTS:<br>

Hematology<br>

The red blood cell count, hemoglobin concentration and hematocrit were below preflight levels in all three crewmen immediately following the Skylab 2 flights. The red cell count showed a gradual recovery by postflight days 4 to 7, but the hematocrit and hemoglobin concentration were below preflight levels during the 18-day postflight period. On Skylab missions 3 and 4, the red cell count and associated measurements were somewhat different from those on  Skylab 2. The red blood cell count, hemoglobin concentration, and hematocrit were increased on recovery as compared to preflight, but began to decrease by postflight day 1, and were significantly lower than preflight values by postflight day 3. On both missions 3 and 4, these hemoconcentration-dependent parameters gradually returned to within preflight limits during the 3-week postflight period. Changes in the plasma volume were thought to be the cause for the alterations in the hemoconcentration-dependent parameters.
<p>
A consistent shift in the specific gravity profile immediately  after flight toward a population of heavier cells was observed. The most dramatic changes were observed after the shorter Skylab 2 flight. On all missions the changes were transient and by postflight day 3, the population distributions of the cells were within preflight ranges. The specific gravity shift was believed to be caused by alterations in red cell membrane lipid content, cell water content, cell electrolyte concentrations, or a combination of all these factors. There were no significant changes in either the active or the passive component of potassium influx following the Skylab 2 flight. However, after Skylab 3 and 4 flights, there were significant elevations in the ouabain-sensitive, metabolism-dependent potassium influx in four of six crewmembers. The increase in potassium influx observed at recovery had returned to normal rates by postflight days 3 to 7. It was speculated that this  change might be a response to transient alteration in the dynamic state of equilibrium existing across the cellular membrane with respect to electrolytes and water.
<p>
Red Cell Shape Classification<br>

Eighty to ninety percent (80-90%) of red cells were classified as discocytes (normal shaped cells) in the preflight phase. After flight, red cells were 83% discocytes, which was not significantly different from preflight percentages. The remaining 15-20% of the nondiscoid cells present during the preflight control phase of each mission consisted primarily of leptocytes, stomatocytes, and knizocytes. During exposure of the crews to the space-flight environment, the frequency of echinocytes increased significantly, and this increase appeared to be related to the duration of each mission. The majority of the echinocytes present in these samples were of the stage I type with few progressing to stages II or III. Postflight samples indicate less than 1% of the population of cells were echinocytes, which was comparable to preflight. Therefore, a rapid reversal of echinocytes to discocytes was observed. The occurrence of echinocytes  was attributed to changes in  plasma, including elevations of plasma lecithin, lysolecithin, and/or free fatty acids.
<p>
